---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ChatHistoryCalculator from '../../components/ChatHistoryCalculator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Chat History Token Calculator" description="Estimate how many tokens your conversation history is using, and how many more turns fit before hitting context limits.">
  <ChatHistoryCalculator client:load slot="tool" />
  <h2>Why chat history grows expensive</h2>
  <p>Most chat APIs are stateless — each request resends the full conversation history. As a chat grows longer, both cost and context-limit risk grow with it, even if each individual message is short.</p>

  <h2>Common Chat History Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Not truncating or summarizing old messages</strong>, letting a long conversation eventually hit the context window limit unexpectedly mid-conversation.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting the system prompt is resent every turn too</strong>, adding to the token cost of every single message beyond just the visible conversation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Chat History Calculator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Estimating cost growth for a long-running chat feature</li>
    <li>Deciding when to implement conversation summarization or truncation</li>
    <li>Planning how many turns a conversation can support before hitting context limits</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Chat History Token FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Does truncating old messages lose context the model needs?</strong> It can — summarizing older turns into a condensed context preserves more useful information than simply dropping them, though both approaches trade off some fidelity for token savings.</p>
  <p><strong>Should I count the system prompt separately?</strong> Yes — since it's resent with every request in a stateless API, its token cost multiplies across the whole conversation, not just once.</p>
</ToolLayout>
